		<category><![CDATA[Tactical Training]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[V-Armed]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[VR training]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.v-armed.com/?p=1754</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Active shooter response is never a solo exercise. It&#8217;s a coordinated, high-stress team event — communication, movement, sector coverage, and split-second decisions made in concert with other officers. Yet a...</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.v-armed.com/2026/09/which-virtual-reality-trainers-best-support-team-based-active-shooter-response-exercises/">Which Virtual Reality Trainers Best Support Team-Based Active Shooter Response Exercises?</a> first appeared on <a href="https://www.v-armed.com">V-Armed</a>.</p>]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p dir="ltr">Active shooter response is never a solo exercise. It&#8217;s a coordinated, high-stress team event — communication, movement, sector coverage, and split-second decisions made in concert with other officers. Yet a lot of VR training systems on the market today are still built around single-trainee, shoot/no-shoot reps. For agencies evaluating VR as part of their active shooter curriculum, the real question isn&#8217;t &#8220;does it look realistic?&#8221; — it&#8217;s &#8220;can my whole team train together, in the same virtual space, and get measurable feedback on how they performed as a unit?&#8221;</p>
<p dir="ltr">Here&#8217;s what to look for.</p>
<p dir="ltr"><strong>1. Genuine free-roaming movement, not fixed positions</strong><br />
A trainee standing still in front of a screen isn&#8217;t rehearsing what officers actually do during an active shooter response — moving to cover, closing distance, clearing a room, redeploying as a threat picture changes. Systems that tether trainees to a stationary point limit training to a narrow slice of the real event.</p>
<p dir="ltr"><strong>2. Real multi-trainee collaboration at scale</strong><br />
The Department of Homeland Security&#8217;s National Urban Security Technology Laboratory (NUSTL) ran an independent market survey of 21 commercial VR training products for first responders. In that comparison, training footprints varied widely — many systems topped out under 1,300 sq ft. V-Armed&#8217;s listed range in the same survey was 900 to 40,000 sq ft, supporting up to 10 trainees who can see, hear, and coordinate with each other inside the same environment. Space matters here: clearing a two-level building with a four-person team requires room to actually maneuver, not a shrunken stand-in for it.</p>
<p dir="ltr"><strong>3. Full-body avatar tracking</strong><br />
Seeing a teammate&#8217;s whole body — not just a floating headset and hands — is what makes squad-based training feel real enough to produce a genuine stress response and build muscle memory officers can draw on later. V-Armed uses a patented full-body inverse kinematic tracking system (FBIKS) with sub-millimeter accuracy, letting trainees see each other&#8217;s posture, positioning, and muzzle direction in real time.</p>
<p dir="ltr"><strong>4. Low latency, to keep the team in the fight</strong><br />
When a system lags behind a trainee&#8217;s actual movement, the result is disorientation or simulator sickness — and a training session cut short. Low motion-to-photon latency isn&#8217;t a nice-to-have for team exercises; it&#8217;s what lets a four- or six-person squad run a scenario start to finish without someone tapping out.</p>
<p dir="ltr"><strong>5. Scenario authoring tied to your agency&#8217;s actual protocols</strong><br />
Canned, pre-scripted scenarios don&#8217;t reflect the buildings, procedures, or threat profiles specific to your jurisdiction. V-Armed&#8217;s Dispatch software lets training staff build and edit unlimited custom scenarios — schools, transit systems, hotels, retail — and layer in AI-driven non-player characters, weather, and lighting changes, so instructors control the objective rather than the vendor.</p>
<p dir="ltr"><strong>6. After-Action Review built for team debriefs</strong><br />
A team exercise needs team-level data: who covered which sector, where communication broke down, whose reaction time lagged. V-Armed&#8217;s AAR system replays the full session from any point of view — including an overhead &#8220;trainer&#8217;s view&#8221; — while capturing reaction time, shots fired, muzzle discipline, and biometric stress indicators for every participant simultaneously.</p>
<p dir="ltr">V-Armed&#8217;s systems are already running team-based active shooter and counterterrorism training at LAPD, the NYPD Counterterrorism Bureau, DHS Federal Protective Service, and LSU&#8217;s National Center for Biomedical Research and Training (NCBRT) — one of DHS&#8217;s own nationally sponsored active shooter training providers. If your agency is evaluating VR for squad-level active shooter response, that&#8217;s the bar to measure against: not just realism, but a system built to scale with your team, your space, and your protocols.</p>
<p data-path-to-node="2">Want to learn how V-Armed Training Solutions have revolutionized officer readiness in cities all across the US? <a href="https://www.v-armed.com/contact/">Contact Us</a></p><p>The post <a href="https://www.v-armed.com/2026/09/which-virtual-reality-trainers-best-support-team-based-active-shooter-response-exercises/">Which Virtual Reality Trainers Best Support Team-Based Active Shooter Response Exercises?</a> first appeared on <a href="https://www.v-armed.com">V-Armed</a>.</p>]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
			</item>
		<item>
		<title>$300M Grant to Invest in Police Readiness</title>
		<link>https://www.v-armed.com/2026/07/300m-grant-to-invest-in-police-readiness/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=300m-grant-to-invest-in-police-readiness</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Paul Grajek]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Tue, 07 Jul 2026 16:47:41 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Defense & Security]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Future of Training]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Virtual Reality Solutions]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[collaborative simulation]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[defense training]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[emergency response]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[homeland security]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[inter-agency coordination]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[massive scalability]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[multi-jurisdictional drills]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Tactical Training]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[V-Armed]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[VR training]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.v-armed.com/?p=1748</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>MODEL CITIES INITIATIVE Making cities safer requires sustained investment in the people who do the work day after day — and in the tools that prepare them for it. Virtual...</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.v-armed.com/2026/07/300m-grant-to-invest-in-police-readiness/">$300M Grant to Invest in Police Readiness</a> first appeared on <a href="https://www.v-armed.com">V-Armed</a>.</p>]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<h2 data-path-to-node="1">MODEL CITIES INITIATIVE</h2>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" data-sourcepos="5:1-5:365;101-465">Making cities safer requires sustained investment in the people who do the work day after day — and in the tools that prepare them for it. Virtual reality training systems that build readiness for every high-risk scenario imaginable, like the V-Armed Training System, are among the most impactful and cost-effective ways municipalities can fulfill that commitment.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" data-sourcepos="7:1-7:504;467-970">On June 3, 2026, the Department of Justice took a significant step in that direction. Through the newly announced <strong>Model Cities Initiative (MCI)</strong>, DOJ is directing nearly $300 million in federal funding to help select cities take a whole-of-city approach to reducing crime and restoring public safety. Two to four cities will be chosen to receive awards supporting comprehensive, innovative public safety strategies — funding that is intended to serve as a model other cities can replicate nationwide.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" data-sourcepos="9:1-9:371;972-1342"><strong>&#8220;This administration is leveraging every authority to ensure the safety of all Americans,&#8221;</strong> said Acting Attorney General Todd Blanche. <strong>&#8220;The Model Cities Initiative will supercharge our law enforcement partners and restore the rule of law to America&#8217;s neighborhoods, towns, and cities. Our message is clear: We will help those who help us Make America Safe Again.&#8221;</strong></p>
<h3 class="text-text-100 mt-2 -mb-1 text-base font-bold" data-sourcepos="11:1-11:37;1344-1380">What the Funding Can Be Used For</h3>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" data-sourcepos="13:1-13:191;1382-1572">MCI dollars are meant to be spent broadly across public safety and behavioral health, and two of the initiative&#8217;s explicitly allowable categories line up directly with what V-Armed delivers:</p>
<ul class="[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-col gap-1 pl-8 mb-3" data-sourcepos="15:1-16:135;1574-1811">
<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2" data-sourcepos="15:1-15:103;1574-1676"><strong>Purchase or lease of equipment, tools, or technology</strong> that reduce crime and restore law and order</li>
<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2" data-sourcepos="16:1-16:135;1677-1811"><strong>Training and professional development</strong> that supports intelligence-led policing, violent crime investigations, and crisis response</li>
</ul>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" data-sourcepos="18:1-18:215;1813-2027">Other eligible uses include hiring and retention, facility costs, mental health and reentry services, victim services, and youth crime prevention — reflecting DOJ&#8217;s holistic, whole-of-city framing of public safety.</p>
<h3 class="text-text-100 mt-2 -mb-1 text-base font-bold" data-sourcepos="20:1-20:19;2029-2047">Who&#8217;s Eligible</h3>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" data-sourcepos="22:1-22:403;2049-2451">Applicants must be local government entities serving a population of at least 100,000. Cities apply through a coordinated, whole-of-city submission — meaning the mayor, sheriff, county prosecutor, and other stakeholders work together on a single proposal for how the funding will be deployed. Proposals are due <strong>September 1, 2026</strong>, and DOJ anticipates making initial award decisions in <strong>late 2026</strong>.</p>
<h3 class="text-text-100 mt-2 -mb-1 text-base font-bold" data-sourcepos="24:1-24:41;2453-2493">Why This Matters for Police Training</h3>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" data-sourcepos="26:1-26:553;2495-3047">Preparing officers for the full range of scenarios they may face in the field — not just firearms qualification, but de-escalation, crisis response, active-shooter response, and high-stress decision-making — is exactly the kind of investment that produces measurable, lasting reductions in risk. Immersive VR training allows departments to run officers through realistic, repeatable, high-risk scenarios without the cost, scheduling burden, or liability of live-action exercises, and to do so at a scale that keeps pace with a department&#8217;s full roster.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" data-sourcepos="28:1-28:229;3049-3277">For cities preparing an MCI proposal, a VR training system is a concrete, fundable line item that speaks directly to the initiative&#8217;s training and technology priorities — and one that demonstrates measurable impact to reviewers.</p>
<h3 class="text-text-100 mt-2 -mb-1 text-base font-bold" data-sourcepos="30:1-30:42;3279-3320">Get Ahead of the September 1 Deadline</h3>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" data-sourcepos="32:1-32:366;3322-3687">If your municipality is considering an MCI application, or simply looking to strengthen your police training curriculum independent of this funding cycle, now is the time to talk. Contact V-Armed to discuss how our training systems can fit into your city&#8217;s whole-of-city safety proposal — and how we can help you make the case that readiness investment saves lives.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" data-sourcepos="34:1-34:127;3689-3815"><em>Learn more about the Model Cities Initiative and application details at <a class="underline underline underline-offset-2 decoration-1 decoration-current/40 hover:decoration-current focus:decoration-current" href="http://www.justice.gov/grants">justice.gov/grants</a>.</em></p>
<p data-path-to-node="2">
<p data-path-to-node="2">Want to learn how V-Armed Training Solutions have revolutionized officer readiness in cities all across the US? <a href="https://www.v-armed.com/contact/">Contact Us</a></p><p>The post <a href="https://www.v-armed.com/2026/07/300m-grant-to-invest-in-police-readiness/">$300M Grant to Invest in Police Readiness</a> first appeared on <a href="https://www.v-armed.com">V-Armed</a>.</p>]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
			</item>
		<item>
		<title>Funding Available &#8211; BJA De-escalation and Crisis Response Training Program FY25</title>
		<link>https://www.v-armed.com/2026/04/funding-available-bja-de-escalation-and-crisis-response-training-program-fy25/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=funding-available-bja-de-escalation-and-crisis-response-training-program-fy25</link>
		
